  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nMuch cooler air mass will overspread the region today into\ntonight in the wake of cold frontal passage. The combination of\ndecent pressure rises amid cold air advection and deep mixing\nshould allow for gusty northwest winds at times today reaching\n25-30 mph. Max temps will reach the upper 60s to lower 70s in \nvalleys, and only 50s to lower 60s across higher higher peaks of\nthe Adirondacks and southern VT.\n\nAs high pressure builds overhead tonight, winds will trend to\ncalm amid the cool and very dry airmass with PWAT's dropping to\n0.40\"-0.50\" (-1 to -2 SD), allowing for strong radiational \ncooling to commence after sunset. Low temperatures by daybreak \nTuesday should fall into the 40s for most areas, except mid to \nupper 30s across portions of the southern Adirondacks/southern \nVT and eastern Catskills. Some patchy frost is expected in the \nmore sheltered areas within the SW Adirondacks and possibly \neastern Catskills. However, coverage potential for frost looks \nisolated at this time, therefore no frost advisory issuance. \nNevertheless, those with sensitive plants/vegetation may want to\ntake precautions in these areas as min temps drop into the \nmid/upper 30s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nTemps should rebound quickly by midweek, as strong mid level \nridging builds into the region. Max temps should reach at least \ninto the 70s, with some possibility (30-70%) for valley areas \nnear and south of Albany to reach near or above 80 Wed/Thu.\n\nA weak cold front approaching from the north could trigger\nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms Wednesday into\nThursday, although there remains uncertainty regarding potential\ncoverage of showers/storms, and also placement of highest\nchances, which will be tied to the actual frontal location which\ncould stall somewhere across the region during this time.",
